WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.888
Part two of lecture eight: sparse matrices.

00:00:03.972 --> 00:00:07.310
A university has 40,000 students

00:00:07.393 --> 00:00:10.648
and 2,500 subjects

00:00:10.731 --> 00:00:13.819
and it wants to store every student's

00:00:13.902 --> 00:00:15.821
score in every subject.

00:00:15.905 --> 00:00:19.326
A two-dimensional array of 40,000

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:22.414
by 2,500 has

00:00:22.497 --> 00:00:24.500
100 million entries.

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:26.802
But each student takes only

00:00:26.875 --> 00:00:28.393
about three subjects.

00:00:28.465 --> 00:00:30.996
So only 120,000

00:00:31.068 --> 00:00:32.804
entries are real scores

00:00:32.876 --> 00:00:34.612
about

00:00:34.684 --> 00:00:36.202
0.12% of the array.

00:00:36.202 --> 00:00:38.010
Everything else is zero.

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:40.779
A streaming company is worse

00:00:40.862 --> 00:00:44.282
10 million users and 10000 movies

00:00:44.365 --> 00:00:47.701
give 10^11 entries.

00:00:47.701 --> 00:00:49.620
At four bytes per int

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:52.289
that is 400 GB

00:00:52.289 --> 00:00:53.790
nearly all zeros.

00:00:53.873 --> 00:00:56.375
A matrix in which most entries

00:00:56.459 --> 00:00:58.794
are zero is a sparse matrix.

00:00:58.794 --> 00:01:01.880
As with the polynomials of part one

00:01:01.963 --> 00:01:03.965
the fix is to store only

00:01:04.049 --> 00:01:05.800
the non-zero entries.

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:07.990
The first solution

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:11.380
store each non-zero entry as a triplet

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:14.346
its row, its column and its value.

00:01:14.431 --> 00:01:16.974
Here is the 5 × 6 matrix

00:01:17.058 --> 00:01:18.414
from the slides.

00:01:18.414 --> 00:01:21.889
We keep the triplets in row-major order

00:01:21.889 --> 00:01:24.940
by row, and by column within a row.

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:28.597
Row zero has a nine in column four

00:01:28.597 --> 00:01:31.243
the triplet (0, 4, 9).

00:01:31.243 --> 00:01:33.511
Row one: (1, 1, 8).

00:01:33.511 --> 00:01:35.873
Row two has two entries

00:01:35.873 --> 00:01:38.047
a four in column zero

00:01:38.047 --> 00:01:40.598
and a two in column three.

00:01:40.598 --> 00:01:43.244
Row three: (3, 5, 5).

00:01:43.244 --> 00:01:45.890
And row four: (4, 2, 2).

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:49.007
Six triplets of three numbers each

00:01:49.007 --> 00:01:50.193
18 ints

00:01:50.193 --> 00:01:52.960
against 30 for the full array.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:55.942
One correction to the slides.

00:01:56.025 --> 00:01:58.427
Their triplet table lists the

00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:01.327
entry of row two as (2, 2, 2).

00:02:01.327 --> 00:02:04.971
But look at row two:

00:02:04.971 --> 00:02:05.966
4 0 0 2 0 0.

00:02:05.966 --> 00:02:07.622
Counting from zero

00:02:07.622 --> 00:02:10.107
the two sits in column three.

00:02:10.107 --> 00:02:13.421
The correct triplet is (2, 3, 2)

00:02:13.421 --> 00:02:16.900
which is exactly what the program prints.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:19.400
Reading one entry means

00:02:19.483 --> 00:02:21.319
scanning the triplets.

00:02:21.402 --> 00:02:25.075
get walks the array and returns the value of

00:02:25.158 --> 00:02:28.663
the triplet with that row and that column.

00:02:28.663 --> 00:02:31.083
Anything not stored is zero.

00:02:31.083 --> 00:02:34.422
So get(2, 3) is 2

00:02:34.422 --> 00:02:37.760
and get(2, 2) is 0.

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:40.391
Because the triplets are sorted

00:02:40.462 --> 00:02:43.100
a binary search can find one in about

00:02:43.171 --> 00:02:44.953
log t steps instead of t.

00:02:45.024 --> 00:02:47.519
And the whole matrix costs 18

00:02:47.591 --> 00:02:49.230
ints instead of 30.

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:52.447
In general, a matrix with r rows,

00:02:52.527 --> 00:02:55.563
c columns and t non-zero entries takes

00:02:55.642 --> 00:02:58.599
3t numbers instead of r × c.

00:02:58.679 --> 00:03:00.916
That pays off when t is less

00:03:00.996 --> 00:03:02.593
than rc/3.

00:03:02.593 --> 00:03:04.191
For the university

00:03:04.271 --> 00:03:07.148
360,000 numbers

00:03:07.227 --> 00:03:09.704
instead of 100 million.

00:03:09.704 --> 00:03:12.900
Of the three solutions in this lecture,

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:15.297
triplets use the least space,

00:03:15.377 --> 00:03:18.333
which is why the slides call them the

00:03:18.413 --> 00:03:20.730
minimum space representation.

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:23.954
The price: reading an entry is a search,

00:03:24.026 --> 00:03:27.475
and inserting a new non-zero entry in the middle

00:03:27.547 --> 00:03:30.206
means shifting the rest of the array,

00:03:30.206 --> 00:03:33.080
just like inserting into an array list.

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:36.229
The second solution puts the same

00:03:36.307 --> 00:03:38.487
triplets into a linked list,

00:03:38.487 --> 00:03:40.744
one node per non-zero entry,

00:03:40.744 --> 00:03:42.768
still in row-major order.

00:03:42.768 --> 00:03:46.194
This matrix has four rows and five columns.

00:03:46.271 --> 00:03:48.840
The method fromArray walks it row

00:03:48.918 --> 00:03:51.799
by row and appends a node at the tail

00:03:51.877 --> 00:03:53.823
for every non-zero value,

00:03:53.823 --> 00:03:56.002
with a dummy node in front,

00:03:56.002 --> 00:03:58.260
as the polynomial merge did.

00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:03.340
Row 0 gives (0, 2, 3)

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:04.692
and (0, 4, 4).

00:04:04.692 --> 00:04:09.084
Row 1 gives (1, 2, 5)

00:04:09.084 --> 00:04:10.661
and (1, 3, 7).

00:04:10.661 --> 00:04:13.139
Row 2 is all zeros,

00:04:13.139 --> 00:04:16.968
so it contributes no node at all.

00:04:16.968 --> 00:04:21.924
Row 3 gives (3, 1, 2)

00:04:21.924 --> 00:04:23.050
and (3, 2, 6).

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:25.800
Inserting or deleting an entry

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:28.200
now just re-links references

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:29.480
nothing shifts.

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:33.080
Each node costs three ints plus a reference

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:36.440
a little more than a triplet in an array.

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:39.598
A single list in row-major order is

00:04:39.671 --> 00:04:41.807
good for walking along a row

00:04:41.807 --> 00:04:44.384
but bad for walking down a column

00:04:44.458 --> 00:04:47.330
the entries of one column are scattered

00:04:47.404 --> 00:04:49.098
through the whole list.

00:04:49.098 --> 00:04:52.264
Matrix algorithms, such as multiplication

00:04:52.264 --> 00:04:55.210
transposition and Gaussian elimination

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:56.020
need both.

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:57.892
An orthogonal list

00:04:57.892 --> 00:05:00.536
also called a cross-linked list

00:05:00.536 --> 00:05:03.501
gives every non-zero node two links.

00:05:03.581 --> 00:05:06.225
right points to the next non-zero

00:05:06.305 --> 00:05:08.068
entry in the same row

00:05:08.148 --> 00:05:11.032
and down points to the next non-zero

00:05:11.113 --> 00:05:13.116
entry in the same column.

00:05:13.196 --> 00:05:16.561
Each row and each column also has a header

00:05:16.641 --> 00:05:19.045
that points to its first node

00:05:19.045 --> 00:05:22.490
an empty row or column has no node at all.

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:23.829
On the slides

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:26.585
the textbook version links the headers

00:05:26.655 --> 00:05:28.422
into a list of their own

00:05:28.422 --> 00:05:31.460
and every row and column list is circular.

00:05:31.531 --> 00:05:33.792
Our Java keeps the idea with two

00:05:33.862 --> 00:05:35.841
arrays of header references

00:05:35.841 --> 00:05:37.678
row head and column head

00:05:37.678 --> 00:05:40.080
and null at the end of each list.

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:42.821
insert makes the new node

00:05:42.821 --> 00:05:45.147
then links it into its row.

00:05:45.147 --> 00:05:48.887
If row r is empty, or its first node lies to

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:50.798
the right of column c

00:05:50.881 --> 00:05:52.875
the new node becomes the

00:05:52.958 --> 00:05:54.786
first node of the row.

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:58.443
Otherwise p walks right while the next node

00:05:58.526 --> 00:06:01.268
is still to the left of column c

00:06:01.268 --> 00:06:04.509
and the new node is linked in after p

00:06:04.509 --> 00:06:06.420
two references change.

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:09.549
The column half is the same code

00:06:09.634 --> 00:06:11.823
with down and row in place

00:06:11.908 --> 00:06:13.592
of right and column.

00:06:13.676 --> 00:06:15.866
So every insert keeps each

00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:17.719
row sorted by column

00:06:17.719 --> 00:06:20.330
and each column sorted by row.

00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:23.930
OrthogonalDemo inserts the six entries

00:06:24.009 --> 00:06:26.155
of the five by four matrix

00:06:26.155 --> 00:06:29.334
then prints every row and every column.

00:06:29.334 --> 00:06:32.036
Row two and column one are empty.

00:06:32.036 --> 00:06:35.215
Now look at the node (1, 3, 3)

00:06:35.215 --> 00:06:37.917
it is the second node of row 1

00:06:37.917 --> 00:06:40.778
and the first node of column 3.

00:06:40.858 --> 00:06:42.685
Every element node sits

00:06:42.765 --> 00:06:44.434
on two lists at once.

00:06:44.513 --> 00:06:47.931
Walking a row or a column now costs only as

00:06:48.010 --> 00:06:51.030
many steps as it has non-zero entries.

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:53.077
Reading entry (r, c)

00:06:53.077 --> 00:06:55.919
walks row r from its header.

00:06:56.011 --> 00:06:58.303
The loop stops as soon as

00:06:58.394 --> 00:07:00.136
it passes column c

00:07:00.136 --> 00:07:03.528
because the row is sorted by column.

00:07:03.528 --> 00:07:05.820
If it lands on column c

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:07.837
it returns the value

00:07:07.837 --> 00:07:10.496
otherwise the entry is zero.

00:07:10.496 --> 00:07:14.530
So the cost is at most the length of row r.

00:07:15.110 --> 00:07:16.134
Now you try.

00:07:16.134 --> 00:07:17.500
In this matrix

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:20.146
which nodes does get(3, 2)

00:07:20.146 --> 00:07:23.047
visit before it returns zero?

00:07:23.133 --> 00:07:25.778
And how many down steps does it

00:07:25.864 --> 00:07:29.534
take to walk column 0, after its header?

00:07:29.534 --> 00:07:32.350
Pause the video and work it out.

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:40.145
get(3, 2) starts at rowHead 3

00:07:40.145 --> 00:07:42.987
the node (3, 0, 8).

00:07:42.987 --> 00:07:44.752
Its column, 0

00:07:44.752 --> 00:07:47.692
is at most 2 but not equal

00:07:47.692 --> 00:07:51.711
so it moves right, to (3, 3, 1).

00:07:51.711 --> 00:07:54.259
Column 3 is past 2

00:07:54.259 --> 00:07:57.788
so the loop stops and returns zero

00:07:57.788 --> 00:07:59.650
two nodes visited.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.656
Column 0 holds (0, 0, 2)

00:08:02.656 --> 00:08:06.226
then (1, 0, 4), then (3, 0, 8)

00:08:06.226 --> 00:08:08.965
two down steps after the header.

00:08:09.048 --> 00:08:11.953
A row-major list would have to walk

00:08:12.036 --> 00:08:14.360
past every entry in between.

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:17.601
Here are the four representations

00:08:17.682 --> 00:08:18.730
side by side.

00:08:18.811 --> 00:08:20.826
The 2D array

00:08:20.907 --> 00:08:22.842
reads any entry at once

00:08:22.842 --> 00:08:25.019
but needs r × c cells.

00:08:25.019 --> 00:08:28.487
The triplet array takes the minimum space

00:08:28.487 --> 00:08:31.389
3t, and binary search reads an

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:34.937
entry in log t steps, but an insert shifts.

00:08:34.937 --> 00:08:38.485
The list of triplets makes an insert cheap

00:08:38.485 --> 00:08:40.501
once the place is found.

00:08:40.582 --> 00:08:43.646
And the orthogonal list pays two links

00:08:43.726 --> 00:08:45.823
per node plus the headers

00:08:45.903 --> 00:08:48.645
but walks any row or any column in

00:08:48.726 --> 00:08:51.306
as many steps as it has entries.

00:08:51.387 --> 00:08:53.644
Which one is best depends on

00:08:53.725 --> 00:08:55.660
the operations you need.

00:08:56.240 --> 00:08:57.067
To sum up.

00:08:57.067 --> 00:08:59.798
One: a sparse matrix stores only

00:08:59.881 --> 00:09:01.618
its non-zero entries.

00:09:01.618 --> 00:09:04.349
Two: triplets in row-major order

00:09:04.431 --> 00:09:06.252
use the minimum space

00:09:06.252 --> 00:09:08.320
three numbers per entry

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:10.968
but an update shifts the array.

00:09:10.968 --> 00:09:13.698
Three: a linked list of triplets

00:09:13.781 --> 00:09:15.436
makes inserts cheap

00:09:15.518 --> 00:09:18.083
and an orthogonal list lets you

00:09:18.166 --> 00:09:20.400
walk any row or any column.

00:09:20.750 --> 00:09:21.586
Try this

00:09:21.679 --> 00:09:25.300
two matrices of the same size are given

00:09:25.392 --> 00:09:28.828
as triplet arrays in row-major order.

00:09:28.828 --> 00:09:29.849
Write add

00:09:29.849 --> 00:09:33.749
which returns their sum in the same form

00:09:33.749 --> 00:09:37.648
in time proportional to t₁ + t₂.

00:09:37.648 --> 00:09:41.362
Hint: it is the polynomial merge again

00:09:41.362 --> 00:09:43.126
with the pair (row, column)

00:09:43.126 --> 00:09:46.190
in place of the exponent.

00:09:46.540 --> 00:09:48.336
Next, in part three

00:09:48.336 --> 00:09:50.940
where two linked lists meet.

